Add a theme toggle to index.html: Day Mode (light blue gradient) and Night Mode (dark starry sky), with smooth CSS transitions.

Design approach:
- CSS custom properties are already in :root. Add a `data-theme="night"` on `<html>` or `<body>`, and override the variables for night mode.
- Night mode: dark background (deep navy), stars. Stars can be generated with JS: randomly place small dots (divs) in a fixed fullscreen container, or use multiple box-shadow on pseudo-elements. Since JS is allowed and embedded, either generate ~120 star spans inside a fixed `#stars` container with random positions, sizes, and twinkling animation, or use canvas. Simplest: JS generates box-shadow on two pseudo layers... even simpler: create divs.
- Smooth transitions: transition on background gradient doesn't animate (gradients don't transition). Options: put two fixed background layers (day layer and night layer) and cross-fade their opacity with transition. That gives smooth transitions. Use `.bg-day` and `.bg-night` fixed layers, and toggle opacity based on `body[data-theme]`.
- Card colors: cards are whitish; at night use dark cards with light text. Colors (var(--ink) etc.) can be transitioned with CSS transitions because colors do transition.
- Toggle button: fixed at top right (or inside the header). Label "🌙 Night Mode" / "☀️ Day Mode". No emoji unless the user asks — the instructions say to only use emoji when the user explicitly requests. A theme toggle usually has an icon, but to follow the rule, avoid emoji; use text "Night" / "Day" or a CSS-drawn sun/moon. Use text labels "☾ Night" / "☀ Day"? ☾ and ☀ are technically not emoji but symbols (some may render as emoji). Safer to use plain text: "Night Mode" / "Day Mode".
- Persist choice in localStorage? Nice-to-have; low cost. Include it (embedded JS, no external). Keep it small.
- Also update tab colors, footer text — these mostly use variables. Override :root variables via body[data-theme="night"]. Card backgrounds also use a var. The accent lightens at night.

Implementation details:

Current body has the gradient background. Refactor: body's background becomes a base color (a color that can transition, e.g., a solid base behind the layers, or a var). Add:

```css
.bg { position: fixed; inset:0; z-index:-1; transition: opacity 0.8s ease; }
.bg-day { background: linear-gradient(...existing...); opacity:1; }
.bg-night { background: linear-gradient(#050814, #0b1530 55%, #16213e); opacity:0; }
body[data-theme="night"] .bg-day { opacity:0; }
body[data-theme="night"] .bg-night { opacity:1; }
```

Stars: inside .bg-night, container #stars with generated star spans; twinkle keyframes. Show only at night (stars are inside .bg-night, so opacity handles it).

Theme-dependent colors via variables:

:root { --ink:#1c2733; --ink-soft:#4a5a6a; --card:rgba(255,255,255,.94); --accent:#1e5fa8; --rule:#d7e3f0; }
body[data-theme="night"] { --ink:#dbe6f4; --ink-soft:#9fb2c8; --card:rgba(16,26,46,0.85); --accent:#7fb3ff; --rule:#2a3c5c; }

Elements use var(--ink) etc. — add `transition: background-color .8s, color .8s, border-color .8s` on body, .col, etc. The body's color uses var(--ink); put `transition: color .8s, background .8s` on * or on specific selectors.

The .lang-tag uses var(--accent)'s background color and white text — no problem; white text on light-blue accent at night may be OK (white on #7fb3ff is so-so). Can set text to dark: give the tag a var(--tag-ink) as color. Simpler: keep it, minor point. Add --on-accent variable: white in day, #0b1530 at night. Used in lang-tag and tabs.active (tabs.active uses #fff background with var(--accent) text — that's fine in both). 

nav.tabs buttons: border is white rgba — on a dark night background, keep it as-is; still visible. The white background of active tab and var accent is fine.

Footer buttons: background #fff color var(--accent) — fine in both themes.

Hint/copyright are white — fine in both (day is blue gradient, night is dark sky).

Toggle button placement: fixed top-right. Style it like the tabs.

